d how you would operationalise the phrase extremely long relative to the attention context window; (e) the statistical plan, including number of independent seeds or rollouts, the comparison test, and how you would handle the case where distributions are non-Gaussian. Keep this at the level of a working sketch, not a finished design. Item 3, consequential assumptions and uncertainties. List every assumption you are forced to make because the source materials are silent on a point. For each assumption, state the assumption, why it is consequential to the design, and what would change if the assumption were wrong. I am particularly concerned about three points: the exact SiT variant and its parameterisation, the precise definition of non-causal in the Hyena operator as applied here, and what baseline trajectory length the original time-attention mechanism is known to handle well. If you cannot resolve any of these from the available materials, say so and propose how the design would adapt. I will review your response carefully before we move to the next stage. Take the time to be thorough; I would rather have a complete and honest inventory than a polished but incomplete one.", "required_handoff_stage": "plan", "approach_approved": false, "public_task": {"question": "Does replacing the time attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in the SiT architecture allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales?", "context": "Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.", "environment": {"runtime": "bubblewrap", "network": "disabled", "repository": "/workspace", "python": "python3", "software_environment": "system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled", "datasets": [], "gpu_enabled": false, "cpu_affinity_count": 224, "note": "Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a comple